= Data Description

The dataset used in this project is the US Traffic Accidents dataset, available on Kaggle, which covers traffic crashes in the United States from 2016 to 2020. The dataset can be accessed at https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/jonbown/us-2020-traffic-accidents/data. This dataset was generated by sampling records from the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration’s (NHTSA) Crash Report Sampling System (CRSS). CRSS itself is designed to provide a nationally representative sample of police-reported motor vehicle crashes in the United States. To develop CRSS, NHTSA uses a probability-based sampling process to select crash reports from law-enforcement agencies in selected geographic areas across the country. The selected police crash reports are then reviewed, interpreted, and coded by trained CRSS coders into standardized electronic data files.

The US Traffic Accidents dataset covers 259,077 crashes from 2016 through 2020 and is provided in CSV format. In 2020 alone, the dataset contains 54,745 crash cases. The records are organized into three main types of files: accident (acc), containing crash-level information; vehicle (veh), containing information about vehicles involved in each crash; and person (pers), containing information about drivers, passengers, pedestrians, and other persons involved. These files can be linked using the common CASENUM variable. Broadly, the dataset contains attributes describing the location and roadway characteristics of the crash, date and time, weather and environmental conditions, crash characteristics and contributing factors, vehicle information, and demographic or injury information for people involved in the crash.

Table 1 summarizes the main attributes that are expected to be relevant to this project; the complete CRSS dataset contains additional variables related to crash, vehicle, and person characteristics.


